WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.488 --> 00:00:13.440
The lobby is where guests meet the wedding parents first, so your posture when greeting them reflects your family's dignity.

2
00:00:13.776 --> 00:00:24.192
Stand with your feet about shoulder-width apart under the skirt — this reduces fatigue during long greeting sessions and keeps your posture stable.

3
00:00:24.528 --> 00:00:32.832
Lightly clasp your hands together and rest them near your lower abdomen for the most graceful look.

4
00:00:33.168 --> 00:00:39.432
Crossing your thumbs slightly gives your hands a neater shape.

5
00:00:39.768 --> 00:00:49.248
When greeting a guest, smile while looking them in the eye, then bow your upper body gently at 15-30 degrees to show respect.

6
00:00:49.584 --> 00:00:58.080
Say something like "Welcome" or "Thank you for coming," then lift your head with a bright smile — that leaves a great impression.

7
00:00:58.416 --> 00:01:04.800
The ease that comes from a well-prepared posture conveys the wedding's warmth to your guests.
